1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,900
An upstream team renames a column.

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:04,340
Nothing errors.

3
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:10,640
Downstream models keep running and quietly
produce wrong numbers, and you find

4
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:14,100
out days later from someone
looking at a bad dashboard.

5
00:00:14,785 --> 00:00:17,525
So let's break it the
way it actually happens.

6
00:00:18,065 --> 00:00:23,765
In DataHub, order play stat becomes
order created at in the real catalog.

7
00:00:24,545 --> 00:00:26,805
Nobody told the downstream teams.

8
00:00:27,410 --> 00:00:31,590
The agent reads DataHub
through the MCP server.

9
00:00:32,290 --> 00:00:38,470
You can watch the calls land,
search, schema fields, lineage

10
00:00:38,690 --> 00:00:41,750
on the changed column, captured queries.

11
00:00:42,695 --> 00:00:44,235
This is not GREP.

12
00:00:44,935 --> 00:00:47,915
Every conclusion is
grounded in the catalog.

13
00:00:48,615 --> 00:00:52,475
Column level lineage gives
three answers, not two.

14
00:00:53,015 --> 00:00:57,675
Three files genuinely reference the
changed column and need a patch.

15
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:03,880
Two Marts sit in the blast radius,
but read an alias created upstream, so

16
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:05,740
they need no change at all.

17
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:08,700
And seven models are correctly skipped.

18
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:14,355
Click a skipped model, and it tells
you why, exactly which columns it does

19
00:01:14,355 --> 00:01:18,535
consume, and that none of them
sit on the changed column's path.

20
00:01:19,315 --> 00:01:21,655
Anything can list everything downstream.

21
00:01:22,435 --> 00:01:25,415
Knowing what genuinely
breaks is the hard part.

22
00:01:25,910 --> 00:01:27,290
Patches are surgical.

23
00:01:27,990 --> 00:01:33,210
SQLOT locates the references and
only the changed tokens move.

24
00:01:33,910 --> 00:01:36,410
The language model never writes code.

25
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:37,930
It writes the explanation.

26
00:01:39,065 --> 00:01:43,565
Then every column reference is
resolved before anything ships.

27
00:01:44,185 --> 00:01:47,085
23 of 23 against the catalog.

28
00:01:47,945 --> 00:01:51,565
One unresolvable reference
blocks the whole patch.

29
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:54,980
The gate is enforced, not advisory.

30
00:01:55,680 --> 00:02:01,460
It opens a real pull request carrying
the column lineage path as evidence

31
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:05,460
and the reason each unaffected
model was left alone.

32
00:02:06,105 --> 00:02:11,225
Then it writes the repair back, corrected
column lineage, the renamed column

33
00:02:11,225 --> 00:02:14,925
documented, tags, and
incident raised for review.

34
00:02:15,545 --> 00:02:19,110
The next engineer or the next
agent inherits all of it.

35
00:02:19,510 --> 00:02:21,930
Column level lineage for impact.

36
00:02:22,790 --> 00:02:25,370
AST rewrites for correctness.

37
00:02:26,150 --> 00:02:29,850
The catalog as the source
of truth on both sides.
